A||||12||Avery Montembeault||Emergency! Need Help ASAP||||1||
Z||000000||Avery Montembeault||12-29-2001||04:21 AM||||Just reccently I put in a new clutch, pressure plate and throw out bearing. I also just replaced my slave cylinder. however, after all these repairs, I cant get the damn thing to shift into any gear while the engine in running! I can shift when the engine is off, with some difficulty though. The master cylinder does not appear to be leaking either. I bled the system repeatadly using archies method, but it still doesnt work, and I have to have the car operational by noon on sunday. Any ideas? or Im I going to have to drop the engine and tranny again and start from scratch (we'll call that plan z...). I have to do all this work by myself, so Im not sure how much my slave is moving.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Joe Torma||12-29-2001||04:40 AM||||If you can't find a helper to push the clutch in, see if you can use a long 2x4 or something.<P>Find out if that slave cylinder is moving.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LZeitgeist||12-29-2001||08:03 AM||||Check out the rechniques/tips in this thread here... but yes, to make use of these techniques, you will need a helper. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016622.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016622.html</A> <P>Good luck... I hope things work out in time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||LZeitgeist||12-29-2001||08:05 AM||||Heck, if I didn't live so far away, <B>I'd</B> come over and help, just having gone through a similar scenario...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Avery Montembeault||12-29-2001||12:10 PM||||how much play should the shift fork have? I can move it a few inches toward the slave cyl, but can only budge it slightly in the direction the slave would be pushing it. Should it be able to move easily, or does the slave have enough power to move it ||||reg||1
Z||000005||LZeitgeist||12-29-2001||12:15 PM||||Since the throwout bearing will be pressed slightly against the clutch fingers, I doubt that you would be able to move the lever by hand at *all* towards the 'released' position (i.e., moving it away from where the slave cylinder sits)... it needs more pressure to move it (which the master/slave cylinder system provides)...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Avery Montembeault||12-29-2001||12:30 PM||||would it be possible that the master cylinder is giving me just enough pressure to bleed the system, but due to defective seals or whatever wont hold enough pressure to move the slave?||||reg||1
Z||000007||LZeitgeist||12-29-2001||12:43 PM||||Yes, that would be a possibility... it takes very little pressure to push the air out of the system, but it takes a solid hydraulic force to push the slave cylinder rod far enough to release the clutch. <P>I know this is frustrating as hell, 'cuz I just went through this, and it took us a week to discover where air was being sucked back into the system. <P>Look through the thread in the link I provided to see if there is anything in there that can help you... that's the best advice I can give. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||West Coast Fiero||12-29-2001||01:22 PM||||possibility of bent clutch pedal?<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000009||Avery Montembeault||12-29-2001||03:18 PM||||I dont know what an unbent one looks like for compairison to determine if it is bent. I dont think it is though, I can push it untill it reaches whatever stop its supposed to hit||||reg||1
Z||000010||StuGood||12-29-2001||04:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B>If you can't find a helper to push the clutch in, see if you can use a long 2x4 or something...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>FWIW, I found that a discarded table leg, 28-3/4" long, worked great for this purpose.  Adjusted the tilt steering wheel full down, laid table leg on driver's seat.  Through side window, pushed clutch pedal in with table leg and wedged top end of table leg under lower edge of steering wheel.  After that, I could walk back to the slave cylinder, and take a "pedal depressed" measurement, without help.<P>When I was ready to verify the slave stroke distance, it was like 2AM! So I thought,<BR>"Rats! It's 2AM & I need to borrow somebody's leg for just a minute..."  ...which made me think of the old table leg.  Worked great!<P>BTW, my neighbor is super nice, and ordinarily he would be willing to come over help with stuff like this.  But, I thought, at this hour, he's liable to put his foot down  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  Or not.<P>Incidentally, after I verified the stroke was sufficient, I wedged the clutch pedal down with the table leg and left it there for like an hour, and measured the slave extension again, to verify no leakdown.  <P>||||reg||3
Z||000011||Bill Shenefelt||12-30-2001||07:40 AM||||The arm that the slave pushes sometimes develops a crack and the movement is in the "bending" of the arm, not movement of the clutch.  Happened on my 86.  Just be sure you do not replace it with a cast arm.  My garage did and it bent fork after fork.  Forks lasted about a year and then failed due to wear and bending. <P>------------------<BR>Bill Shenefelt||||reg||1
Z||000012||Hairy_Fiero||12-30-2001||08:26 AM||||What year Fiero? If it is a pre 87 Fiero you may have an aluminum clutch pedal that is bent. Check the clutch pedal with a magnent to determine if it is steel or aluminum. Won't stick if it's aluminum but will if it's steel. If it's aluminum it's a good bet it's bent and you should replace it with a later model steel pedal/banjo arm. Good Luck.||||reg||3
